{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DtYC6VMVucewj96NnnMbtx","type":"tool_result","content":"   1 ---\n   2 phase: 01-retry-budget\n   3 reviewed: 2026-11-02T11:52:35Z\n   4 depth: deep\n   5 files_reviewed: 4\n   6 files_reviewed_list:\n   7   - probe/__init__.py\n   8   - probe/budget.py\n   9   - tests/test_budget.py\n  10   - devflow.toml\n  11 findings:\n  12   critical: 0\n  13   warning: 10\n  14   info: 7\n  15   total: 17\n  16 status: issues_found\n  17 ---\n  18 \n  19 # Phase 1: Code Review Report\n  20 \n  21 **Reviewed:** 2026-11-02T11:52:35Z\n  22 **Depth:** deep\n  23 **Files Reviewed:** 4 source and config files, plus every planning document in `git diff main...HEAD`\n  24 **Status:** issues_found\n  25 \n  26 ## Summary\n  27 \n  28 **No finding is rated Critical, but one reviewer rated one finding Critical and this merge overruled it. See the severity ruling below.** The code matches the plan and the suite passes (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v`: exit 0, `Ran 2 tests`, `OK`). No secret or session data is in the branch. No artifact claims a push, PR, merge, tag or human approval that did not happen.\n  29 \n  30 The main concerns are:\n  31 - the 120-second value is still unconfirmed by a human, and some documents read as if it were settled;\n  32 - the tests do not enforce the constraint the source comment states;\n  33 - the planning documents contradict each other or their own git history in several places.\n  34 \n  35 This report replaces the earlier standard-depth review and keeps its four finding IDs (WR-01, WR-02, IN-01, IN-02) for the same four findings.\n  36 \n  37 ### Severity ruling on the agent-chosen budget (WR-02)\n  38 \n  39 The generalist pass rated WR-02 Critical because the plan's Task 1 is `gate=\"blocking-human\"`, no human answered it, and the agent's choice of 120 stands. The doc-accuracy and external-state passes rated the same facts Warning.\n  40 \n  41 This merge rates it **Warning**, for these reasons:\n  42 - The reviewer rubric reserves Critical for security vulnerabilities, data loss, crashes and authentication or authorization bypasses in the code. This code has none of those, and it meets BUDGET-01 as written.\n  43 - The claim that the PR would make the decision \"look human-approved\" did not hold up. SUMMARY, VERIFICATION, UAT and STATE all record it as an agent decision.\n  44 - The gate is not bypassed. VERIFICATION is `human_needed`, `verification.status` returns `human_needed`, and the ship workflow refuses that status.\n  45 \n  46 **What this ruling does not change:** the human decision is still open. Nothing in this review confirms that 120 is right for the real upstream.\n  47 \n  48 ### Scope and method\n  49 \n  50 - GSD's incremental scope was empty, because only planning files changed since the previous review. It would have skipped the review, so the scope was set by hand to the whole phase.\n  51 - Planning documents, normally excluded, were included because the doc-accuracy and external-state angles need them.\n  52 - Five finder passes ran in parallel: doc accuracy, security and leaked data, CI and build correctness, external-state claims, and a generalist deep pass. The merge re-checked the timestamps, ignore rules, `Deviations` text, NaN behaviour and verification status directly. Finder results for mutants and remote state are reported as they ran them.\n  53 \n  54 ### What was checked and came back clean\n  55 \n  56 - **Secrets:** `gitleaks git --redact` (10 commits) and `gitleaks dir` found no leaks. A separate `rg` pass over `git log -p main..HEAD` found no credentials, home paths or personal emails. A planted path and a planted fake token both tripped the `rg` patterns. Pattern scanners can still miss a secret in an unusual format.\n  57 - **External claims:** `git ls-remote origin` shows only `main` and `integration`. The PR, tag and release listings are empty. `feature/phase-01` has not been pushed.\n  58 - **False green:** there is no CI, and no documented command uses a pipe or `|| true`. The documented command failed visibly on import errors, a missing directory and a different working directory.\n  59 \n  60 ### Not established\n  61 \n  62 - Whether the 90-second failover and 240-second pager figures are true. They appear only in the plan.\n  63 - The \"DevFlow resume instruction\" the artifacts cite as the basis for the agent's choice. It is not in the repository.\n  64 - What reads `devflow.toml`, and which of the two base-branch settings wins.\n  65 - `covered_digest` in VERIFICATION was not recomputed.\n  66 - The mutation results come from 15 hand-picked mutants on one interpreter, not an exhaustive run.\n  67 \n  68 ## Warnings\n  69 \n  70 ### WR-01: Constraint stated as fact in code is unenforced; tests accept 30 and 300\n  71 \n  72 **File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`, `tests/test_budget.py:5,10-12`\n  73 **Issue:** The comment says the budget must cover the 90-second failover and stay under the 240-second pager. The tests only assert membership in `(30, 120, 300)`. In a throwaway copy, constants of 30 and 300 both exit 0 with `OK`, while 45, 120.0, True, -120 and 0 fail. A change to 300, which the comment says is wrong, would ship green. The plan's must-haves only ask for membership, so this is a gap in the plan rather than an executor fault.\n  74 **Fix:** Assert the bounds in a test, with their source noted, and do not pin 120 until a human confirms it:\n  75 ```python\n  76 FAILOVER_SECS, PAGER_SECS = 90, 240  # plan figures, unverified\n  77 self.assertGreaterEqual(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, FAILOVER_SECS)\n  78 self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, PAGER_SECS)\n  79 ```\n  80 The plan forbids editing `tests/test_budget.py`, so this needs a follow-up plan or a new test file.\n  81 \n  82 ### WR-02: Unverified, agent-chosen value documented as settled\n  83 \n  84 **File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`; gate at `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-PLAN.md:10,29`\n  85 **Issue:** Plan Task 1 is `gate=\"blocking-human\"` with `autonomous: false`. No human answered it, and an agent chose 120. `01-UAT.md` shows `result: [pending]`, with 0 passed and 1 pending. The planning documents say this plainly, but the source comment does not: it states both figures as fact and gives no sign that the value is provisional, and it is the only record a later maintainer is likely to read. The reasoning holds on the plan's figures, since 30 is below 90, 300 is above 240, and only 120 fits. The figures themselves are unchecked.\n  86 **Fix:** A human who knows the real upstream and pager must answer UAT test 1 before merge. Until then, mark the comment provisional:\n  87 ```python\n  88 # PROVISIONAL: agent-chosen, awaiting human confirmation (see 01-UAT.md).\n  89 # Plan figures, not independently verified: must cover a 90 s upstream failover\n  90 # and stay under the 240 s on-call pager escalation.\n  91 ```\n  92 \n  93 ### WR-03: BUDGET-01 is marked Complete while its sign-off is open\n  94 \n  95 **File:** `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5,12`, against `01-VERIFICATION.md:84` and `01-UAT.md:22,29`\n  96 **Issue:** REQUIREMENTS shows `[x]` and `Complete` with no qualifier. VERIFICATION says \"SATISFIED in code; the choice itself awaits human confirmation\". The requirement's literal text is met, so the checkbox is defensible, but the traceability row is where someone deciding on a merge would look, and it omits the open item.\n  97 **Fix:** Qualify the row, for example `Complete in code; value pending human confirmation (01-UAT.md)`, or leave it pending until UAT test 1 is answered.\n  98 \n  99 ### WR-04: SUMMARY reports no deviations and gives an inconsistent basis for the agent's authority\n 100 \n 101 **File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md:86,89,94`, against `01-UAT.md:23` and `01-VERIFICATION.md:30`\n 102 **Issue:** Line 94 says \"None - plan executed exactly as written\", while line 86 says an agent answered a `blocking-human` gate. Line 89 bases the decision on a standing instruction about tool-marked sign-offs, which does not obviously cover a plan's choice among options. UAT and VERIFICATION base it on DevFlow's resume instruction instead. The repository can check neither basis. Line 89's \"without any preference involved\" also overstates the case, because the plan lists a real worker-slot cost for 120.\n 103 **Fix:** Record the agent's resolution of Task 1 as a deviation, give one basis across all documents, and replace the \"preference\" wording with \"30 and 300 are excluded by the plan's figures; the worker-slot cost of 120 was accepted\".\n 104 \n 105 ### WR-05: Review and verification timestamps are later than the commits that contain them\n 106 \n 107 **File:** `01-VERIFICATION.md:3,24,111`; the earlier version of this report\n 108 **Issue:** VERIFICATION says `verified: 2026-11-02T12:10:00Z`, but its commit `8ddef7a` is dated 11:46:37Z, about 23 minutes earlier. `01-UAT.md` names VERIFICATION as its source yet says it started at 11:46:36Z. The earlier review had the same fault (`12:00:00Z` in a commit dated 11:44:22Z), and this report replaces it. The round values look typed rather than measured. They do not affect the merge decision, but other tools and readers use these fields to order events.\n 109 **Fix:** Write these fields from `date -u +%FT%TZ` when the file is generated, or derive them from the commit.\n 110 \n 111 ### WR-06: `devflow.toml` and `.planning/config.json` name different base branches, and a PR to main adds the file\n 112 \n 113 **File:** `devflow.toml:1`, `.planning/config.json`\n 114 **Issue:** `devflow.toml` says `base_branch = \"integration\"`; `.planning/config.json` says `\"base_branch\": \"main\"`. This branch is built on `integration` (`ef2d757`), and `main` has no `devflow.toml`, so a PR to `main` adds a file that points tooling at a different branch than GSD uses. No phase document mentions the file. `capture_retention = 64` is also unexplained.\n 115 **Fix:** Decide what `main` should contain, then either make the two settings agree or record why they differ. Pass an explicit `--base` when opening the PR.\n 116 \n 117 ### WR-07: The deadline test leaves realistic mutants alive\n 118 \n 119 **File:** `tests/test_budget.py:12`\n 120 **Issue:** The single sample `deadline_after(10.0)` does not catch `int(start_secs) + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`, `10.0 + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` or a hard-coded `130.0`. All three exit 0. The `int()` mutant is the most realistic, because it silently drops sub-second precision. Twelve of the 15 mutants tried were killed.\n 121 **Fix:** In a new test file, sample several non-integral starts, then confirm the three mutants fail:\n 122 ```python\n 123 for start in (0.0, 0.25, 1234.5):\n 124     self.assertEqual(deadline_after(start) - start, DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS)\n 125 ```\n 126 \n 127 ### WR-08: A NaN start time removes the retry bound\n 128 \n 129 **File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n 130 **Issue:** `deadline_after(float('nan'))` returns `nan`, and both `nan < x` and `nan >= x` are False. A loop that stops on `now >= deadline` would therefore never stop. `deadline_after(True)` returns 121. No caller exists yet, so the impact is latent. A clock source does not normally return NaN, so this matters only if the value comes from external data.\n 131 **Fix:** Reject non-finite input with `math.isfinite`, or state the requirement in the docstring when the first caller is written.\n 132 \n 133 ### WR-09: Runtime state is not reliably kept out of git\n 134 \n 135 **File:** `.gitignore:1-4`, `.planning/state.json`, `.planning/milestone.lock`\n 136 **Issue:** `.planning/state.json` is untracked and matches no ignore rule, and `git add -n .planning` would stage it. Its content is harmless today: a command hint and a timestamp. `.planning/milestone.lock` holds a Claude Code session id and a PID, and is hidden only by `.git/info/exclude`, which a fresh clone does not have. Neither file is in any branch commit.\n 137 **Fix:** Add `.planning/state.json` and `.planning/milestone.lock` to the tracked `.gitignore`, or track `state.json` on purpose after reviewing what it holds.\n 138 \n 139 ### WR-10: Zero discovered tests only fail on Python 3.12 or newer, and no interpreter is pinned\n 140 \n 141 **File:** repository as a whole; verify command at `01-01-PLAN.md:67`\n 142 **Issue:** With an empty `tests/`, a misnamed file or a class that is not a `TestCase`, the command exited 5 on Python 3.15.2. That exit status was added in 3.12, so older interpreters would exit 0, and nothing pins the version. An all-skipped suite exits 0 on every version, though no test skips today. The repository has no CI, so these runs are manual.\n 143 **Fix:** Pin the interpreter (for example `.python-version`) to 3.12 or newer, and assert the test count if the command is ever scripted.\n 144 \n 145 ## Info\n 146 \n 147 ### IN-01: `deadline_after` does not say which clock `start_secs` is on\n 148 \n 149 **File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n 150 **Issue:** The result is only meaningful against the same clock as the input. Mixing `time.monotonic()` and `time.time()` gives a silently wrong deadline, and wall-clock time can step.\n 151 **Fix:** State in the docstring that both values must come from one clock, and recommend `time.monotonic()`.\n 152 \n 153 ### IN-02: Docstring leaves open whether the deadline bounds an in-flight call\n 154 \n 155 **File:** `probe/budget.py:9`\n 156 **Issue:** A call started just before the deadline with no per-call timeout could run past 240 seconds, which would defeat the comment's stated purpose. No caller exists yet.\n 157 **Fix:** State that callers must cap each attempt at the remaining budget, or add a per-attempt timeout when the retry loop is written.\n 158 \n 159 ### IN-03: STATE.md labels the phase several ways\n 160 \n 161 **File:** `.planning/STATE.md:5,14,23`\n 162 **Issue:** The frontmatter says `status: verifying` and `percent: 0`, the body says `EXECUTING`, and the next line says \"Phase complete — ready for verification\". None of these should be read as a pass.\n 163 **Fix:** Let GSD regenerate the file once the UAT item is answered.\n 164 \n 165 ### IN-04: VALIDATION's wording can be read as a human sign-off\n 166 \n 167 **File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md`\n 168 **Issue:** It shows `status: validated`, `nyquist_compliant: true`, `Gaps found 0` and an \"Approval\" line, alongside an open manual item and known test blind spots. The approval is labelled as an automated audit, so this is not a false claim. The `created` date also changed from 2026-09-28 to 2026-11-02 without comment; the new date matches the git history.\n 169 **Fix:** Word it as \"automated audit only; no human sign-off. Open: UAT test 1.\"\n 170 \n 171 ### IN-05: STATE reports resume-only time as the plan's duration\n 172 \n 173 **File:** `.planning/STATE.md`, against `01-01-SUMMARY.md:49,60`\n 174 **Issue:** STATE records \"1 min\", but SUMMARY says that covers only the resume and that Task 1's time was not measured.\n 175 **Fix:** Add the qualifier or leave the cell blank.\n 176 \n 177 ### IN-06: No CI or repository-level secret scanning\n 178 \n 179 **File:** repository as a whole\n 180 **Issue:** The repository has no CI workflow, tracked pre-commit hook or dependency manifest. Tests and secret scans run only when someone runs them by hand.\n 181 **Fix:** If this repository outlives the probe, add a tracked `gitleaks` pre-commit hook and a workflow that runs the test suite.\n 182 \n 183 ### IN-07: Tests depend on the working directory and repeat the offered values\n 184 \n 185 **File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5`, `tests/`\n 186 **Issue:** `tests/` has no `__init__.py`, and imports resolve only when run from the repository root; running elsewhere fails visibly rather than passing. The offered values also appear in the test, REQUIREMENTS and the plan, so a human choosing ano