ow.toml` hold only workflow toggles, `base_branch`, and `capture_retention`.\n\n## What this does not establish\n\n- The `rg` and gitleaks passes look for pattern and entropy matches only. They cannot detect a secret in an unusual format, or a base64 or otherwise encoded value. I did not decode anything.\n- Only the local refs were scanned. The `origin` remote's other refs (if any exist beyond `main` and `integration`) were not fetched or inspected. I did not check whether the remote repository is public.\n- Runtime files are written by GSD and DevFlow tooling that I did not run. I looked at the files that exist now. Other tool actions (for example `capture_retention = 64` in `devflow.toml` suggests captured session data under `.devflow/`) may write other content; `.devflow/` does not exist in this worktree, so I could not inspect it. It is ignored by the tracked `.gitignore`, so it travels safely.\n- I did not review `probe/budget.py` or the tests for logic defects. That is outside this angle.\n##### ci-build\n\n# Phase 01: CI / Build Correctness Review\n\n**Angle:** Can a failing step still report green?\n**Scope:** read-only worktree; all mutants ran in a scratch copy (`git archive HEAD`), since removed.\n\n## Summary\n\nThere is no CI in the tree, so nothing runs the verify command automatically. The command is the only gate, and it is `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v`, with no pipe, so its exit code is the real one. It exits 0 on the unmodified tree on every interpreter I could run. Several wrong-code and wrong-test states also exit 0.\n\n## What exists (measured)\n\n- `git ls-files` has no `.github/`, no `.gitlab-ci.yml`, no Makefile, no tox/nox/pyproject/setup.cfg, no `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. The only automation is outside the tree: `core.hooksPath=~/.config/git/hooks`, whose `pre-commit` runs gitleaks and then chains to `.git/hooks/pre-commit` of the common git dir. That hook does not run the tests, and a fresh clone does not get it.\n- `devflow.toml` (`base_branch = \"integration\"`, `capture_retention = 64`) contains no verify or test command.\n- `.planning/config.json` says `base_branch: \"main\"` and `devflow.toml` says `integration`. This is a config disagreement, not a CI issue (see IN-02).\n\n## Interpreters actually run\n\nCPython 3.11.16, 3.12.15, 3.13.14, 3.13.18, 3.14.8, 3.14.10, 3.15.2 (via mise installs, brew and /usr/bin). Baseline command: exit 0, `Ran 2 tests ... OK` on all of them. NOT run: 3.8, 3.9, 3.10 (not installed; the `mise` shims for 3.11/3.12 are unset and error). The code uses nothing version-specific beyond annotations, so I expect those to pass, but that is unverified.\n\n## Negative-control results (exit codes, documented command, unmodified except for the mutation)\n\nBehaves correctly (non-zero, as it should):\n\n| Mutation | Exit |\n|---|---|\n| broken import in test (`probe.budgetx`) | 1 |\n| `deadline_after` renamed | 1 |\n| syntax error in `probe/budget.py` | 1 |\n| `tests/` directory deleted | 1 (`Start directory is not importable`) |\n| constant = 45, 90, 240, 250, 0, -1, 121, 120.0, `\"120\"`, True | 1 |\n| deadline `start - budget` | 1 |\n\nReports green while the code or the test suite is wrong (exit 0):\n\n| Mutation | Py 3.11 | Py 3.12 to 3.15 |\n|---|---|---|\n| constant = 30 or 300 (the source comment forbids both) | 0 | 0 |\n| every test skipped (`@unittest.skip` or `setUp` calling `skipTest`) | 0, prints `OK (skipped=2)` | 0, same |\n| test file renamed to `budget_test.py` (no longer matches `test*.py`) | **0**, `Ran 0 tests OK` | 5, `NO TESTS RAN` |\n| tests/ present but no test file | **0**, `Ran 0 tests OK` | 5 |\n| test class no longer a `TestCase`, or methods renamed off `test_` | **0**, `Ran 0 tests OK` | 5 |\n| `deadline_after` returns `10.0 + budget` (hard-coded start) | 0 | 0 |\n\nNot defects (control confirming the harness): renamed to `testbudget.py` still matches `test*.py`, exit 0, 2 tests ran. Removing `probe/__init__.py` still exits 0 (namespace package); it is harmless.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: The test accepts 30 and 300, which the source comment forbids\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5,9-11` and `probe/budget.py:3-4`\n**Issue:** The comment in `budget.py` says the budget must cover 90 s and stay under 240 s, so only 120 qualifies among the offered values. `OFFERED_BUDGETS = (30, 120, 300)` is what the test checks, so constants 30 and 300 pass with exit 0 (measured on 3.11 to 3.15). The test checks \"is one of the offered options\", not the documented constraint. The VALIDATION sign-off already concedes it cannot tell 30, 120 and 300 apart, but still sets `nyquist_compliant: true` and `status: validated`. A green result therefore does not mean the comment's invariant holds.\n**Fix:** Assert the invariant the comment states, and keep it separate from the offered-values check. Example: `self.assertGreater(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, 90); self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, 240)`. Name the 90 and 240 constants in `budget.py` and import them in the test. These figures are unconfirmed per `01-UAT.md`, so the test should say it is checking the stated assumption, not the truth.\n\n### WR-02: Zero collected tests exits 0 on Python 3.11 and below\n\n**File:** `01-VALIDATION.md` (Quick/Full run command), `01-01-PLAN.md:66-70`\n**Issue:** `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` returns 0 with `Ran 0 tests ... OK` on 3.11 when the test file is renamed, absent, or not collected. The exit code 5 for \"no tests ran\" only exists from 3.12. The command is written as `python3`, and the interpreter version is pinned nowhere (no `.python-version`, no pyproject `requires-python`, no CI matrix). On this machine `/usr/bin/python3` is 3.14.8 and the mise `python3` shim is 3.15.2, so `python3` resolves differently depending on PATH; a host with older Python reports green on an empty suite.\n**Fix:** Make the gate independent of the interpreter: pin a minimum version and assert the count, e.g. `python3 -c 'import sys; assert sys.version_info >= (3,12)' && python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v`, or run `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v 2>&1 | tee /dev/stderr | rg -q '^Ran [1-9]'` with `pipefail` set, or add a sentinel test that fails when fewer than 2 `RetryBudgetTest` tests exist.\n\n### WR-03: A skip-all state reports `OK` with exit 0\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:8` (class), verify commands in `01-01-PLAN.md:67` and `01-VALIDATION.md`\n**Issue:** Skipping both tests via `@unittest.skip` on the class, or `skipTest` in `setUp`, prints `OK (skipped=2)` and exits 0 on every interpreter tested. The plan's acceptance wording (\"reports `Ran 2 tests` and `OK`\") is also satisfied by `Ran 2 tests ... OK (skipped=2)`. The exit-code gate cannot see this and no check inspects for skips.\n**Fix:** Add `-W error` only helps for warnings, not skips. Use a runner check such as `python3 - <<'PY'` that loads the suite with `unittest.TextTestRunner`, then `sys.exit(0 if r.wasSuccessful() and not r.skipped and r.testsRun >= 2 else 1)`, or ban skip decorators with `rg -n 'skip' tests/` in the same command. At least tighten the plan wording to `OK` with no `skipped=`.\n\n### WR-04: No automated gate exists outside the author's machine\n\n**File:** repository root (absent `.github/`, hooks config, Makefile)\n**Issue:** The \"sampling rate\" in VALIDATION (\"after every task commit\", \"before verify-work\") is a procedure, not an enforced step. Nothing in the tree invokes the tests on commit, push or PR. The one hook that does exist is in the user's home directory, runs gitleaks only, and is not versioned. A red suite can be committed and pushed with a normal commit. This is the \"failing step can still report green\" case in the strongest form: the step is never run.\n**Fix:** Raise as a decision for the operator, not an automatic change: add a repo CI workflow (pinned Python version, runs the command above, fails on 0 tests) and optionally a versioned pre-commit hook. Per the user's supply-chain policy, a missing check is a hard failure, so the absence should be recorded as an open item rather than treated as covered.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: Hard-coded-start mutant survives\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:14-15`\n**Issue:** `deadline_after` returning `10.0 + budget` passes (exit 0) because the test uses only the start value `10.0`. VALIDATION already admits this. Recorded here because it is a measured exit 0 on wrong code.\n**Fix:** Parametrize over at least two start values (for example `0.0`, `10.0`, `1234.5`), or use `subTest`.\n\n### IN-02: Base-branch settings disagree\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1` vs `.planning/config.json` (`git.base_branch: \"main\"`)\n**Issue:** The two files name different base branches (`integration` vs `main`). It is not a build failure here, but any tool that reads one of them to pick a diff base or a merge target will behave differently from one that reads the other. The harness environment gives `main` as the PR base.\n**Fix:** Operator decision on which is authoritative; make the other agree.\n\n## What these checks do not establish\n\n- They show the exit code of one command under single mutations. They do not show the code is correct: whether 120 is the right budget depends on the 90 s and 240 s figures, which nothing in the repo confirms.\n- Python 3.8 to 3.10 were not run. The 3.11 \"Ran 0 tests OK\" result is measured, and I expect it to hold on 3.8 to 3.10, but I did not measure that. Whether the 3.12 exit-5 behaviour appears on 3.12.0 itself is not measured (3.12.15 only).\n- Each mutation was applied one at a time. Combined mutations were only spot-checked (removing the assertions plus constant 45 gives exit 0, as expected).\n- Whether the real `pre-commit` hook would block a commit was not run (a commit would modify the repo). I read its contents only.\n- The previous `01-REVIEW.md` was not relied on.\n\n##### external-state\n\n# Phase 1: External-State Claims Review\n\nLive state checked 2026-11-02 ~12:03Z: `git ls-remote origin` lists only `main` (c7f624a) and `integration` (ef2d757). `git tag` is empty. `gh pr list --state all --json` returns `[]` (exit 0). `gh release list` is empty (exit 0). `feature/phase-01` (f633cf0) is not on the remote. Merge-base of main and HEAD is c7f624a; merge-base of integration and HEAD is ef2d757 (integration is an ancestor of HEAD, one commit ahead of main).\n\nNo commit message or document in main..HEAD claims a merge, push, tag, PR, release or deletion. Nothing was found to contradict live remote state. One claim is a human action (the operator decision); it is not checkable from here.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: UAT `## Summary` counters contradict the test result and the commit message\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-UAT.md:17,22-27`\n**Issue:** Test 1 says `result: pass` and frontmatter `status: complete`, and commit d230b4e is titled \"1 passed, 0 issues\". The Summary block still reads `passed: 0`, `pending: 1`. `git show d230b4e` shows the commit edited the frontmatter, Current Test and test result but left the counters untouched. Anything that reads the counters (not the per-test result) sees one pending item. The commit-message claim is contradicted by the file it describes.\n**Fix:** Set `passed: 1` and `pending: 0` (leave `total: 1`).\n\n### WR-02: \"Decided by the operator\" is an unevidenced human-action claim, and the recorded result contradicts its own text\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-UAT.md:17-18`\n**Issue:** Classification: not checkable from here (neither confirmed nor contradicted). Repository evidence: the only author in main..HEAD is \"DevFlow Probe\"; the UAT edit (d230b4e, 12:00:26Z) was made 14 minutes after the pending UAT (8ddef7a, 11:46:37Z) and contains no quote, transcript reference, or message ID of the operator's answer. \"Recorded by the orchestrating session\" means the same agent that made the original 120 choice is the one asserting the human approved it. Separately, the test's `expected:` is that the upstream's failover and pager figures are confirmed; the resolution says \"This project has no real upstream or pager ... This does not establish anything about a real system.\" So `result: pass` records a pass against an expectation the resolution says was not tested. Nothing is proven false; but a downstream reader cannot tell a real operator sign-off from an agent self-approval.\n**Fix:** Quote or cite the operator's actual instruction (session/message reference), or reword the test's `expected:` to what was actually decided (\"120 accepted as the fixture default; real-world figures not checked\") so `pass` matches it. The orchestrator should confirm with the operator whether this decision was theirs.\n\n### WR-03: VERIFICATION status flipped in frontmatter only; body and human_verification block still say human_needed\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VERIFICATION.md:4,15-18,25,92-102`\n**Issue:** Commit f633cf0 changed exactly one line: `status: human_needed` to `status: passed` (line 4). Still unchanged: line 25 `**Status:** human_needed`; the frontmatter `human_verification:` list (lines 15-18) still carries the open item; \"Human Verification Required\" (92-98) and \"Gaps Summary\" (102: \"Status is `human_needed` solely because...\") still describe the item as open. Line 30 and 96 still state no human answered it. The file now disagrees with itself, and the document was not re-verified; the status was changed by a one-line edit after a UAT edit 17 seconds earlier.\n**Fix:** Update the body status, move the human item into a resolved note referencing the UAT resolution (and WR-02's caveat), and update Gaps Summary; or revert the status until that is done.\n\n### WR-04: VERIFICATION `verified:` timestamp is in the future relative to every commit that contains it\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VERIFICATION.md:3,24,111`\n**Issue:** `verified: 2026-11-02T12:10:00Z` (also line 24 and 111). The file was first committed in 8ddef7a at 11:46:37Z (06:46:37-05:00), 23 minutes before the stated verification time. The status flip commit f633cf0 is at 12:00:43Z and the system clock at review time was ~12:02Z, so the value was still in the future after the flip and was not refreshed. A rounded, invented timestamp undermines the \"verified in this session\" evidence claims.\n**Fix:** Use the actual verification time and bump it on re-verification/status change.\n\n### WR-05: STATE, ROADMAP, REQUIREMENTS and state.json disagree on whether the phase is done\n\n**File:** `.planning/STATE.md:3-13,22-27`, `.planning/ROADMAP.md:13-15`, `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5,12`, `.planning/state.json`\n**Issue:** ROADMAP marks `1/1 plans executed` with `[x]`; REQUIREMENTS marks BUDGET-01 `[x]` and `Complete`. STATE.md says `status: verifying`, `completed_phases: 0`, `percent: 0`, `last_updated 11:42:13Z`, \"Phase complete — ready for verification\", and its Decisions entry still ends \"Needs human confirmation.\" even though UAT now claims confirmation. `.planning/state.json` is untracked, last updated 11:42:12Z, and routes to \"Advance to the next step (verify)\" at 0%. Verification passed, UAT complete, yet STATE was not updated after 11:42Z. Requirement marked Complete while its own decision text says unconfirmed.\n**Fix:** Update STATE after UAT/verification (status, progress, decision note referencing the UAT resolution) and regenerate or exclude state.json.\n\n### WR-06: A PR to main carries the integration-only `devflow.toml` commit and a conflicting base_branch\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1-2`, `.planning/config.json` (`git.base_branch: \"main\"`)\n**Issue:** HEAD is built on `integration` (ef2d757), which is one commit ahead of main and exists on the remote. `git diff main...HEAD` therefore adds `devflow.toml` (`base_branch = \"integration\"`, `capture_retention = 64`) to main. A PR targeting main would land a file that points tooling at `integration` while `.planning/config.json` on the same branch says `main`. A PR to `integration` would not carry that commit. No phase document mentions the file or the target branch.\n**Fix:** Decide the PR target before shipping. If main, drop ef2d757 from the PR or reconcile the two base_branch settings; if integration, make sure no one retargets to main.\n\n### WR-07: Review disposition record is stale versus the committed review that VERIFICATION cites\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW-DISPOSITION.md:4-20`, `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VERIFICATION.md:104`\n**Issue:** Disposition (recorded 11:44:42Z) lists 4 findings (WR-01, WR-02, IN-01, IN-02), all open. The review committed later in 8a03c38 (reviewed 11:52:35Z) reports 17 findings (10 warning, 7 info). VERIFICATION line 104 repeats \"4 items, all open\". The disposition for the 13 additional findings does not exist, and VERIFICATION's statement of open findings is understated.\n**Fix:** Re-run the disposition gate against the current 01-REVIEW.md and correct the VERIFICATION count.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: Remote-state claim in the existing review is currently true\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md:57`\n**Issue:** The claim that only `main` and `integration` exist on origin, with empty PR/tag/release listings and an unpushed feature branch, matches live state as checked above. This confirms the claim at this moment only; it does not guard against later changes.\n**Fix:** None.\n\n### IN-02: The review's \"five finder passes ran in parallel\" claim is not checkable\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md:52`\n**Issue:** There is no artifact in the repository for the other passes. Classified not checkable; not treated as false.\n**Fix:** None required; optionally link the pass outputs.\n\n### IN-03: VALIDATION `created:` date was rewritten\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md:6`\n**Issue:** `created` changed from `2026-09-28` (as on main) to `2026-11-02`. A creation date should not move; the original is part of main's history. Minor provenance loss.\n**Fix:** Restore `2026-09-28` or add an `updated:` field.\n\n##### generalist\n\n# Phase 01: Generalist Deep Pass\n\nMethod: read the plan and BUDGET-01 first, then the five files, then ran the tests and mutants in a `git archive HEAD` copy (since removed). Only after forming findings did I skim the headings of the earlier 01-REVIEW.md.\n\nCode vs plan: `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120` and `deadline_after` returning `start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` match the plan and BUDGET-01. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` reports `Ran 2 tests ... OK`. I found no functional bug in the code that exists.\n\n## Mutants (run in the scratch copy; baseline OK)\n\n| Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `120` -> `30` | SURVIVES (suite OK) |\n| `120` -> `300` | SURVIVES (suite OK) |\n| `start + BUDGET` -> `start + 120` literal | SURVIVES (see WR-01) |\n| `120` -> `90` (not an offered value) | killed |\n| `+` -> `-` | killed |\n| `return start_secs` | killed |\n| `120` -> `120.0`, `120` -> `True` | killed (isinstance check) |\n\nNegative control: the baseline passes and four of the seven mutants fail, so the harness does detect change. It does not establish that these are the only relevant mutants; it is a hand-picked set of 7.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Tests do not pin the value; swapping 120 for 30 or 300 passes\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5,10-12`, `probe/budget.py:5`\n**Issue:** The only value assertion is membership in `(30, 120, 300)`. Changing the constant to 30 (below the 90 s failover the comment in `budget.py:3-4` says must be covered) or 300 (above the 240 s pager limit it says must not be exceeded) leaves the suite green; I demonstrated both. The deadline test computes its expected value from the same constant, so it is circular for the value. The comment states the constraint as fact but nothing enforces it, so a later edit that violates it passes CI.\n**Fix:**\n```python\nFAILOVER_SECS, PAGER_SECS = 90, 240\ndef test_budget_covers_failover_and_precedes_pager(self) -> None:\n    self.assertGreater(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, FAILOVER_SECS)\n    self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, PAGER_SECS)\n```\n(or `assertEqual(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, 120)` if the exact value is to be pinned). Use a literal expected value in the deadline test, e.g. `assertEqual(deadline_after(10.0), 130.0)`, if 120 is pinned.\n\n### WR-02: Provenance of the chosen value is contradictory across the phase records\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-PLAN.md:10` (gate `blocking-human`, `autonomous: false`); `01-01-SUMMARY.md:29,86` (\"made by the orchestrator agent ... NOT by a human ... no human answered it\"); `01-UAT.md:18` (\"Decided by the operator on 2026-11-02 ... recorded by the orchestrating session\")\n**Issue:** The plan makes the budget a blocking human decision. SUMMARY says no human answered it; UAT, written later, records it as an operator decision. I cannot tell from the repository which is true. The code is consistent with the plan's own constraints (120 is the only offered value inside 90..240), so this does not make the code wrong, but it decides whether the human gate was satisfied, and that matters for merging a phase whose plan said `autonomous: false`. The value is also justified only by figures that appear in the plan itself; nothing here verifies them against a real upstream.\n**Fix:** Have the operator confirm or change the value in one line of a tracked artifact, and make SUMMARY and UAT agree. Until then treat 120 as provisional.\n\n### WR-03: `main...HEAD` carries repo config and 11 commits of planning history beyond the code change; the config names a base branch other than the one this merges into\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1`, `.planning/config.json` (`git.base_branch: \"main\"`)\n**Issue:** `git diff main...HEAD` adds `devflow.toml` (`base_branch = \"integration\"`, `capture_retention = 64`) from commit `ef2d757`, and this branch sits on `integration`, which already contains it (`git diff main integration` is exactly that file). Merging this branch into `main` therefore lands DevFlow tooling config on `main` that tells DevFlow `main` is not the base, while `.planning/config.json` in the same tree says `main`. The two disagree. Merging into `integration` is clean. If the intended target is `main`, the code change is `ef2d757` excluded: `probe/__init__.py`, `probe/budget.py` (plus the pre-existing test). `capture_retention = 64` has no consumer in this repository and I could not check what DevFlow does with it.\n**Fix:** Decide the target. If `main`, take the code commit (`2784ea8`) without `ef2d757`, or reconcile the two base-branch settings first. If `integration`, no action on this item.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: No input validation: NaN, infinity, negative and bool starts are accepted\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n**Issue:** Demonstrated: `deadline_after(float('nan'))` -> `nan`; `float('inf')` -> `inf`; `-500.0` -> `-380.0`; `True` -> `121`; `'a'` -> `TypeError`. A `nan` deadline makes `now < deadline` and `now >= deadline` both false, so a naive retry loop would be unbounded or never start depending on which form it uses. No caller exists in this repo, so this is not a demonstrated defect, only a contract gap.\n**Fix:** When a caller is added, validate with `math.isfinite`, or document that the caller owns validation.\n\n### IN-02: Clock and in-flight semantics unspecified\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n**Issue:** The docstring says \"the time at which retries stop\" without saying which clock `start_secs` uses (monotonic vs wall) or whether the deadline interrupts a call already in flight. Using `time.time()` for the start and `time.monotonic()` for the check would yield meaningless results with no error.\n**Fix:** One docstring sentence, e.g. \"`start_secs` must come from `time.monotonic()`\".\n\n### IN-03: Test discovery depends on the invocation form and the working directory\n\n**File:** `tests/` (no `__init__.py`), `tests/test_budget.py:3`\n**Issue:** Only `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` from the repo root works. `python3 -m unittest discover` (no `-s`) prints `Ran 0 tests ... NO TESTS RAN`; `cd tests && python3 -m unittest test_budget` fails with an import error. The result is only a failure on the Python version here for the zero-test case if the exit code is non-zero (I did not capture the exit code; unverified). `OFFERED_BUDGETS` also duplicates the plan's list rather than the constraint (WR-01).\n**Fix:** Add `tests/__init__.py` or document the one supported command.\n\n### IN-04: `.planning/state.json` is untracked and not ignored\n\n**File:** `.gitignore:1-4`\n**Issue:** `git status` shows `?? .planning/state.json` and `git check-ignore` does not match it, while `.devflow/` is ignored. A `git add -A` would commit runtime state. Nothing has done so yet.\n**Fix:** Add `.planning/state.json` to `.gitignore` if it is runtime state.\n\n## Comparison with the earlier 01-REVIEW.md (headings only; its other claims not verified)\n\n- Independently confirmed: tests accept 30/300 (its WR-01 = my WR-01, with mutants run); NaN and the clock/in-flight gaps (its WR-08, IN-01, IN-02 = my IN-01, IN-02; I rate NaN Info, not Warning, because no caller exists); `devflow.toml` vs `.planning/config.json` base-branch mismatch (its WR-06 = my WR-03); `.planning/state.json` not ignored (its WR-09 = my IN-04); cwd-dependent test discovery (its WR-10, IN-07 = my IN-03).\n- Not confirmed by me and out of scope for this pass: its claims on timestamps, STATE.md wording, VALIDATION wording, CI and secret scanning, and REQUIREMENTS \"Complete\" status. I did not check them.\n\n## What this review does not establish\n\n- Whether 120 s is right for any real upstream; the 90 s and 240 s figures come only from the plan.\n- Which Python version the project targets; I used the host `python3` and did not record its version.\n- Behavior of DevFlow itself (`devflow.toml` keys), or any caller of `deadline_after`; none exists in this repository.\n- Mutation coverage is 7 hand-made mutants, not an exhaustive tool run.\n\n_Reviewer: Claude (generalist deep pass)_\n","numLines":476,"startLine":1,"totalLines":476}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S1rVDtgqEiQgtFFL3PzjGw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
