he two values the source comment rules out (WR-01).\n\nThis report replaces the previous one. Findings that carry over keep their IDs, and new findings start at WR-11 and IN-08. IN-06 is narrowed because its CI half is now WR-11.\n\n### Severity ruling on the human gate (WR-02, WR-13)\n\nAll five passes rated these Warning, and this merge agrees. The reviewer rubric reserves Critical for security vulnerabilities, data loss, crashes and authentication or authorization bypasses. These findings concern planning records, not the code, and the code meets BUDGET-01 as written.\n\n**What this ruling does not establish:** that the human gate was satisfied. The previous report rested its ruling partly on `verification.status` returning `human_needed`, so that the ship workflow would refuse. **That safeguard no longer holds**: the query now returns `passed`, because it reads a frontmatter field that a one-line edit changed. Having no Critical finding means only that this rubric was not triggered.\n\n### Scope and method\n\n- GSD's incremental scope was empty, because only planning files changed since the previous review (`check evaluation-scope --since`: `status: degraded`, `reason: no-task-commits-since`). It would have skipped the review, so the scope was set by hand to the whole phase. The resolver's phase-wide scope lists only `probe/__init__.py` and `probe/budget.py`; `tests/test_budget.py`, `devflow.toml` and `.gitignore` were added from its `outsideUnion` list.\n- Planning documents, normally excluded, were included because the doc-accuracy and external-state angles need them.\n- Five finder passes ran in parallel: doc accuracy, security and leaked data, CI and build correctness, external-state claims, and a generalist deep pass. Each was given a different scope and instructions, and none had to accept the previous report.\n- The merge re-ran directly: the test suite, the verification status query, gitleaks with a planted control, and the constant, hard-coded-start, skip-all and empty-suite controls below. It re-read the cited lines. Other results are reported as the finders measured them.\n\n### What was checked and came back clean\n\n- **Secrets:** `gitleaks git --redact --log-opts=main..HEAD` (12 commits) and `gitleaks dir --redact .` exit 0. A planted fake key and token in a scratch directory made `gitleaks dir` exit 1, so the scanner can fire. A separate `rg` pass over the tree and `git log -p main..HEAD` found no credentials, home paths, usernames or personal emails, and a planted sample tripped its patterns. All 12 commits use `DevFlow Probe <devflow-probe@localhost>`. Pattern scanners can still miss encoded or unusually formatted secrets.\n- **External claims:** at review time, `git ls-remote origin` showed only `main` and `integration`. `git tag`, `gh pr list --state all` and `gh release list` were empty. `feature/phase-01` was not on the remote.\n- **`covered_digest`:** recomputed with `gsd-tools verification fingerprint` and identical to VERIFICATION's value.\n- **Code against plan:** `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120` and `deadline_after` match the plan and BUDGET-01.\n- **Exit codes:** the documented command has no pipe, so its exit code is the real one. It exits non-zero on a broken import, a syntax error, a deleted `tests/` directory, a renamed function, and constants such as 45, 120.0 and True.\n\n### Not established\n\n- Whether the 90-second failover and 240-second pager figures are true of anything. They appear only in the plan.\n- Who the \"operator\" in `01-UAT.md:18` is, or whether that decision was made.\n- The \"DevFlow resume instruction\" and \"standing global instruction\" the artifacts cite as authority. Neither is in the repository.\n- What reads `devflow.toml`, which of the two base-branch settings wins, and what `capture_retention` does.\n- Python 3.8 to 3.10 behaviour. Python 3.11 to 3.15 were run.\n- Exhaustive mutation coverage. All mutants were hand-picked, and no mutation tool was run.\n- Whether the remote repository is public, or whether the out-of-tree pre-commit hook blocks a commit. Its contents were read, but it was not triggered with a planted secret.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Constraint stated as fact in code is unenforced; tests accept 30 and 300\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`, `tests/test_budget.py:5,10-11`\n**Issue:** The comment says the budget must cover the 90-second failover and stay under the 240-second pager. The tests only assert membership in `(30, 120, 300)`, and the deadline test computes its expected value from the same constant. In a scratch copy, constants of 30 and 300 both exit 0 on Python 3.11 to 3.15, while 45 exits 1. A change to a value the comment forbids would ship green. The plan's must-haves only ask for membership, so this is a gap in the plan rather than an executor fault.\n**Fix:** Assert the bounds in a new test file, because the plan forbids editing `tests/test_budget.py`. Note the source of the figures:\n```python\nFAILOVER_SECS, PAGER_SECS = 90, 240  # plan figures, not independently verified\nself.assertGreater(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, FAILOVER_SECS)\nself.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, PAGER_SECS)\n```\n\n### WR-02: Who chose the budget is contradictory, and UAT passes a test its own text says was not performed\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-UAT.md:16-18`, `01-01-SUMMARY.md:86`, `01-VERIFICATION.md:30`, `01-01-PLAN.md:10,29`, `probe/budget.py:3-4`\n**Issue:** There are three separate problems.\n- **Provenance:** the plan makes the choice `gate=\"blocking-human\"` and `autonomous: false`. SUMMARY and VERIFICATION say an agent chose 120 and no human answered. UAT, edited later, says \"Decided by the operator on 2026-11-02 ... recorded by the orchestrating session\". The edit carries no quote or message reference, and its author is `DevFlow Probe`. This claim is **not checkable from here**: nothing confirms or contradicts it, and it is not shown to be false. A reader cannot tell an operator sign-off from an agent recording its own approval.\n- **Result against expectation:** `expected` is that the upstream failover and the pager have the stated timings. The resolution says no real upstream or pager exists and \"This does not establish anything about a real system\", yet `result` is `pass`. The previous version of this line said \"this test is not marked passed\".\n- **Premise:** UAT says the project has no real upstream or pager. VERIFICATION (lines 17, 94), VALIDATION (line 55) and SUMMARY (line 90) still call for confirmation against \"the real upstream\", and the source comment states both figures as fact.\n**Fix:** The operator confirms or corrects the decision in one line of a tracked artifact. Then reword `expected` to what was actually decided (for example, \"120 accepted as the fixture default; real-world figures not checked\") and use one premise in every document, including the source comment.\n\n### WR-03: BUDGET-01's records disagree on whether confirmation is still open\n\n**File:** `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5,12`, `.planning/STATE.md:33`, `01-VERIFICATION.md:84`, `01-VALIDATION.md:55`, against `01-UAT.md:17`\n**Issue:** REQUIREMENTS shows `[x]` and `Complete`, and UAT shows `pass`. STATE's decision ends \"Needs human confirmation\", VERIFICATION's table says \"the choice itself awaits human confirmation\", and VALIDATION still sends readers to an \"Open item in `01-UAT.md`\". A person deciding on a merge gets a different answer depending on which file they open.\n**Fix:** Once WR-02 is settled, make all of these say the same thing, and let GSD regenerate STATE.\n\n### WR-04: SUMMARY reports no deviations and gives an inconsistent basis for the agent's authority\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md:86,89,94`, against `01-VERIFICATION.md:30`\n**Issue:** Line 94 says \"None - plan executed exactly as written\", while line 86 says an agent answered a `blocking-human` gate. Line 89 bases the decision on a s